From ea138281fdd9626dbaaac9ac6e26287eeb24ce51 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ernest Borowski <ernest.borowski.git@proton.me>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 16:02:25 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] ntfs: write through the page cache when direct I/O hits a
 hole

A direct I/O write into a hole allocates the clusters in
ntfs_write_da_iomap_begin_non_resident(): ntfs_attr_map_cluster() puts
the new cluster numbers into the runlist, and the data reaches them
only when the bios complete.  Buffered reads that do not take the inode
lock (page faults, splice()), and any read once an asynchronous write
has dropped it, map the new clusters in that window and read what they
held before, data of deleted files.  The partial clusters at the start
and the end of the write are zeroed on disk only after the runlist
shows them, which opens the same window for the parts the write does
not cover; a reader can leave the old data in the page cache there.
initialized_size covers the write before it is issued, so appending
writes are affected as well.

Reproducer: fill the free space of a volume with a marker pattern and
delete it, create a sparse file, and fill its holes with O_DIRECT
writes while other processes read the clusters being written through
new mappings and splice() after dropping the page cache.  The marker
shows up within the first two writes, with 4 KiB and with 64 KiB
clusters.

NTFS has no unwritten extents to hide the clusters until the data is
there, so do what ext2 and gfs2 do: ->iomap_begin() returns -ENOTBLK
for a hole instead of allocating, and ntfs_dio_write_iter() writes the
rest of the request through the page cache with delayed allocation,
then writes it back and invalidates the page cache as before.
Overwrites of allocated clusters stay direct.  The only clusters direct
I/O still allocates are those of a delalloc run whose folios are gone
(writeback failed to allocate it); zero them on disk before the runlist
shows them, like a write fault does, instead of zeroing only the partial
clusters at the edges after they are mapped.

Fixes: b041ca562526 ("ntfs: update iomap and address space operations")
Cc: stable@vger.kernel.org
